# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: release-artefact-set-incomplete — the published release set is missing 7 of the 10 required artefacts
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-release-artefact-set

* Context

pjb, 2026-08-16, named the artefact set a release must publish:

#+begin_example
clautolisp-${release}-binaries-linux-x86-64.tar.bz2
clautolisp-${release}-libraries-linux-x86-64.tar.bz2
clautolisp-${release}-binaries-darwin-arm64.tar.bz2
clautolisp-${release}-libraries-darwin-arm64.tar.bz2
clautolisp-${release}-binaries-windows-x86-64.tar.bz2
clautolisp-${release}-libraries-windows-x86-64.tar.bz2
clautolisp-${release}-documentation.tar.bz2
clautolisp-${release}-sources.tar.bz2
clautolisp-${release}-sources.zip
clautolisp-${release}-all.tar.bz2      -- includes the contents of all the above
#+end_example

This blocks =release-1.9.0=: cutting the tag today publishes an
incomplete set.

* Problem

Verified by RUNNING the real =collect-artefacts= over a fabricated
complete set of per-target inputs, not by reading the recipe. Given all
nine per-target artefacts plus sources and documentation, the combined
set it writes is:

#+begin_example
clautolisp-1.8.40-binaries.tar.bz2                    <- merged, multi-platform
clautolisp-1.8.40-binaries-windows-x86-64.tar.bz2
clautolisp-1.8.40-documentation.tar.bz2
clautolisp-1.8.40-libraries.tar.bz2                   <- merged, multi-platform
clautolisp-1.8.40-libraries-windows-x86-64.tar.bz2
clautolisp-1.8.40-sources.tar.bz2
clautolisp-1.8.40-sources.zip
#+end_example

Three independent causes, worth separating because they need different
fixes:

** C1 — the per-platform unix artefacts are merged away (4 files)

=collect-artefacts= UNIONS every =binaries-*= into one
=clautolisp-<ver>-binaries.tar.bz2= and every =libraries-*= into one
=clautolisp-<ver>-libraries.tar.bz2=, then passes through only
sources, documentation and =*windows*=. So
=binaries-linux-x86-64=, =libraries-linux-x86-64=,
=binaries-darwin-arm64= and =libraries-darwin-arm64= exist as
per-JOB artefacts but never reach the combined set.

The merge itself is correct — verified that the union really is a union
(each target's marker survives), not a last-writer-wins overwrite. The
defect is that the per-target inputs are consumed rather than also
published.

** C2 — the Windows lane is a STUB (2 files)

=release:windows:x86-64= (.gitlab-ci.yml) does not package anything: it
probes the toolchain and writes =dist/windows-x86-64-STUB.txt=. So
=binaries-windows-x86-64= and =libraries-windows-x86-64= cannot be
produced at all today. The =*windows*= passthrough in
=collect-artefacts= is ready for them; there is simply nothing to pass.
The intended work is =documentation/windows-package-spec.md=.

The GitHub lane has the same stub (=.github/workflows/release.yml=,
"Build + package (Windows)").

** C3 — the =-all= bundle does not exist (1 file)

Nothing anywhere writes =clautolisp-<ver>-all.tar.bz2=. Confirmed by
grep over the root Makefile, =.gitlab-ci.yml=, =scripts/= and
=.github/workflows/=: zero matches for =all.tar.bz2=. It is not
mis-wired, it was never implemented.

* Two further findings, same audit

- *arm32 is built and silently dropped.* =release:linux:arm32= produces
  per-target artefacts, but =collect:release='s =needs:= lists only
  linux:x86-64, linux:arm64, darwin:arm64, windows:x86-64 and
  documentation. The arm32 artefact therefore never reaches the collect
  job. Either add it to =needs:= or stop building it — today the
  pipeline pays for a build whose output is discarded without a word.

- *GitHub's collect job is an explicit TODO.* It unions nothing; it
  downloads every artefact and attaches them as-is. So the two forges
  publish DIFFERENT sets from the same tag. Per AGENTS.md, GitLab is the
  source of truth, but the divergence should be deliberate rather than
  accidental.

* Proposal

1. =collect-artefacts=: pass the per-target =binaries-<os>-<arch>= and
   =libraries-<os>-<arch>= through to =COLLECT_OUT= in addition to
   writing the merged pair. Cheap: they are already in =COLLECT_IN=.
2. Add =release:linux:arm32= to =collect:release='s =needs:= (or drop
   the lane), so nothing is built and discarded.
3. Implement =clautolisp-<ver>-all.tar.bz2= in =collect-artefacts=,
   containing every other artefact of the set. Decide first WHAT it
   contains: the artefact FILES verbatim (an archive of archives —
   simple, verifiable, what "includes the contents of all the above"
   most directly means), or their unpacked union (one tree that installs
   everything at once). These are different products; pjb to arbitrate.
4. Windows packaging per =documentation/windows-package-spec.md= — the
   only item here that is real work rather than plumbing, and the one
   that gates two of the ten files.

* RESOLVED except C2 — 2026-08-16

pjb's rulings: add linux-arm32 *and any future runner's binaries*; the
=-all= artefact is an UNPACKED UNION (one tree that installs everything
at once); a 1.8.x release is cut today, so prepare the CI.

Done:

- *C1* — =collect-artefacts= now passes every per-target
  =binaries-<os>-<arch>= / =libraries-<os>-<arch>= through to the
  combined set, *by glob*, alongside the merged multi-platform pair. A
  new runner's artefact joins the set with no edit here. The old special
  case that copied =*windows*= through is gone, subsumed by that glob —
  it also used to copy the Windows lane's STUB =.txt= into the release.
- *C3* — =clautolisp-<ver>-all.tar.bz2= is built as the unpacked union
  of every per-target artefact plus documentation and sources.
  =sources.tar.bz2= unions safely because it unpacks under its own
  =clautolisp-<ver>/= prefix, alongside rather than into =bin/=, =lib/=,
  =libexec/= and =share/=. =sources.zip= is deliberately left out: same
  content as the tarball, so it would duplicate the source tree.
- *arm32* — added to =collect:release='s =needs:=, so a full build is no
  longer discarded in silence.
- Two checks, both PROVEN to fail before being trusted.
  =scripts/check-release-artefact-set.sh= fabricates a complete input
  set, runs the REAL recipe and diffs the produced file list against the
  specified one — breaking the passthrough made it name the ten lost
  files and exit 1. =scripts/check-release-collect-needs.sh= fails when
  a =release:*= job is missing from =collect:release='s =needs:=; it
  caught arm32 on its first run, before the fix. Wired as
  =make check-release= and run by a new =check:release= CI job.
- That job is deliberately NOT folded into =documentation=, which is
  skipped on tags — exactly the pipeline where a release is cut. It runs
  in the =test= stage, so a tag pipeline fails before the whole release
  matrix builds.
- =collect-artefacts= now prints which targets actually reached it. A
  short set is then visible in the log instead of being silently
  smaller.

*C2 — IMPLEMENTED 2026-08-16, shakedown pending.* pjb: « J'ai construit
les zip windows avec make release sur windows sans probleme (a partir
d'un bash msys2) », so the lane runs the ordinary Makefile from bash
instead of reimplementing packaging in PowerShell.

- =pack-release-artefact= chooses the CONTAINER by target: =.zip= for
  windows, =.tar.bz2= elsewhere. So =make release-programs= produces the
  right artefact on the box with no Windows-specific recipe.
- The zip branch would otherwise ship having NEVER RUN anywhere but
  Windows, so =scripts/tests/test-pack-release-artefact.sh= forces the
  target OS and drives BOTH branches on a Unix host. A typo in the zip
  branch now fails on Linux, not on the release runner.
- =scripts/release-windows.sh= does the build+package and ASSERTS the two
  expected zips exist before exiting, so a build that produced nothing
  fails in the lane that owns it rather than in collect.
- The PowerShell → MSYS2 handoff follows run-forest-run
  (=poseidon:/home/pjb/src/fabrik=, pointed at by pjb):
  =scripts/avec-bash.ps1= + =scripts/environnement-windows.sh= are adapted
  from it. Reading it corrected a real defect in my first draft — I had
  defaulted =MSYSTEM= to UCRT64 because that is what the reference box
  has installed, whereas =build-libredwg.sh= REFUSES UCRT64 and CLANG64
  for the native codec. Details in build-tool-paths-unquoted.issue.
- Two silent-failure modes closed: PowerShell does not fail a step on a
  native program's non-zero exit (the job checks =$LASTEXITCODE=, without
  which a failed build would go GREEN and the release would come up two
  artefacts short), and =git-bash.exe= is avoided — it is a GRAPHICAL
  launcher that detaches and returns at once.

SHAKEDOWN PENDING: like the vendor probes before it, the lane needs its
first run on the real runner to settle. =avec-bash.ps1= could not be
executed here (no PowerShell on poseidon); =environnement-windows.sh= WAS
executed, and its non-Windows paths behave.

Still open on C2: nothing in code, only the first real run — plus the
=build-libredwg.sh= guard noted in build-tool-paths-unquoted.issue, which
protects any caller that does NOT go through these scripts.

** Windows format and -all containers — SETTLED 2026-08-16 (pjb)

*Windows ships =.zip=, not =.tar.bz2=.* The conflict below is therefore
resolved in favour of the Windows-native format. The collect side is
already wired for it: =clautolisp-<ver>-{binaries,libraries}-windows-*.zip=
pass through and join the =-all= union, but are deliberately NOT fed to
the merged multi-platform =binaries.tar.bz2= / =libraries.tar.bz2= —
those are a =$PREFIX=-shaped unix tree, and the Windows package has its
own layout. Keeping it out is the point, not an oversight.

*=-all= ships in BOTH containers*: =clautolisp-<ver>-all.tar.bz2= and
=clautolisp-<ver>-all.zip=, "pour nos amis windowsiens". Same union
tree, two containers. The check verifies BOTH are complete unions —
checking only the tarball would let a half-built zip reach the very
users it exists for.

=collect-artefacts= ERRORS when =zip= or =unzip= is missing while a
Windows artefact is present, instead of skipping: a release must not
quietly ship without the artefacts a whole platform depends on. Both CI
jobs' package guards now install them.

The expected set is therefore *17* artefacts: 8 unix per-target
(4 targets x binaries/libraries), 2 Windows zips, the 2 merged unix
tarballs, documentation, sources.tar.bz2, sources.zip, and the 2 =-all=
containers.

** A specification conflict to settle before C2 is implemented

The artefact list asks for
=clautolisp-<ver>-binaries-windows-x86-64.tar.bz2= and
=...-libraries-windows-x86-64.tar.bz2= — the same shape as every other
target, a =$PREFIX=-shaped =tar.bz2= pair.

=documentation/windows-package-spec.md= specifies a DIFFERENT product:
=<App>-<version>-win-<arch>.zip=, exactly one top-level =<App>\=
directory, Windows-native layout (=bin/=, =lib/common-lisp/=, =doc/=),
app-local CRT, no absolute path anywhere.

SETTLED above: pjb chose =.zip=. What remains of C2 is the packaging
work itself — producing those two zips on the Windows runner per
=windows-package-spec.md=. One question survives for whoever does it:
the spec describes ONE application zip
(=<App>-<version>-win-<arch>.zip=, single top-level =<App>\=), while the
artefact list keeps the binaries/libraries SPLIT that every other target
has. The collect side currently expects the split
(=...-binaries-windows-x86-64.zip= + =...-libraries-windows-x86-64.zip=);
if the single app zip is wanted instead, say so and the expected set
changes by one line in =check-release-artefact-set.sh=.

Consequence for the 1.8.x release cut today: the Windows lane is still a
stub, so the set will contain *14 of the 16* artefacts. Every other
target is complete.

* Acceptance criteria

1. On a =release-*= tag, =collect:release='s artefact contains all ten
   files named above, with the version substituted.
2. Verified the way this issue was: run =collect-artefacts= over a
   complete input set and DIFF the produced file list against the ten
   names — a check that can fail, not an eyeball.
3. Nothing is built by the release pipeline whose output does not reach
   the collect job.
4. =make check-versions= still passes.
